    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d994">

                <pb facs="0963" n="705" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg705"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 841.731/323</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Great
                        Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-02-10T18:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 10, 1915, 6 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1107. Your 1291, December 18.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d994-f1"/> Department
                    receiving many protests from commercial organizations trading with Smith
                    American countries against position of British Government in refusing to permit
                        <pb facs="0964" n="706" xml:id="pg706"/> telegrams in code passing between
                    the United States and certain South American countries. Please bring this
                    situation to attention of Foreign Office, pointing out that telegrams to and
                    from United States and Argentine Republic and other South American countries are
                    messages between neutral countries entirely outside war zone. The matter of
                    coding messages to South America is one of greatest importance to American
                    commercial houses and one in which this Government is deeply interested. Urge
                    early reply.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bryan</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
